= 1I
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Number Theory}

= Solution
{parent=1I}

A <reduced positive definite binary quadratic form> $f=[a,b,c]=ax^2+bxy+cy^2$ satisfies
$$
|b|\leq a\leq c,
$$
with $b\geq0$ when $|b|=a$ or $a=c$. Its <discriminant of a binary quadratic form> is $d=b^2-4ac<0$. The first displayed inequality already gives $|b|\leq a$, while $c\geq a$ gives
$$
|d|=4ac-b^2\geq4a^2-a^2=3a^2.
$$
Taking the positive <square root> yields
$$
\boxed{|b|\leq a\leq\sqrt{|d|/3}.}
$$
Moreover, $d=b^2-4ac\equiv b^2\equiv b\pmod2$, so $b\equiv d\pmod2$. For fixed negative $d$, the displayed bounds leave only <finite set>[finitely many] possible <integers> $a$ and $b$, and then $c=(b^2-d)/(4a)$ is uniquely determined. Hence only finitely many reduced forms have discriminant $d$.

For $d=-15$, the bound gives $a\leq\sqrt5$, so $a\in\{1,2\}$. The <modular congruence> condition makes $b$ <odd number>[odd]. If $a=1$, then $b=\pm1$ and $c=4$; the boundary convention $|b|=a\Rightarrow b\geq0$ retains $[1,1,4]$. If $a=2$, then $b=\pm1$ and $c=2$; the boundary convention $a=c\Rightarrow b\geq0$ retains $[2,1,2]$. Thus the complete list is
$$
\boxed{[1,1,4],\qquad [2,1,2].}
$$

= 2H
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Topics in Analysis}

= Solution
{parent=2H}

<Nash's theorem> says that every finite game, including a two-player game with two pure choices each, has at least one <Nash equilibrium> in <mixed strategies>.

For player $1$, holding $p_2$ fixed, the <expected value> of the payoff is
$$
E_1=-Up_1p_2+2p_1(1-p_2)-2(1-p_1)p_2+(1-p_1)(1-p_2)
=1-3p_2+p_1\bigl[1-(U-1)p_2\bigr].
$$
It is an <affine function> of $p_1$. Therefore player $1$ chooses $p_1=1$ when $1-(U-1)p_2>0$, chooses $p_1=0$ when it is negative, and is indifferent when it vanishes. By <symmetry>, player $2$ has the analogous <best response>.

If $U<2$, then $1-(U-1)p>0$ for every $p\in[0,1]$, so becoming a Dark Lord with probability one is a <strictly dominant strategy>. The unique equilibrium is
$$
\boxed{(p_1,p_2)=(1,1).}
$$
If $U>2$, each player is indifferent when the other chooses $p=1/(U-1)$. This gives one interior mixed equilibrium, while the two asymmetric pure equilibria arise because a player facing a certain peasant chooses Lord and a player facing a certain Lord chooses peasant. The three equilibria are
$$
\boxed{(1,0),\qquad(0,1),\qquad
\left(\frac1{U-1},\frac1{U-1}\right).}
$$
Consequently $\boxed{U_0=2}$. At the threshold itself there is a continuum of equilibria with $p_1=1$ or $p_2=1$, consistent with the question's strict inequalities.

= 3G
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Coding and Cryptography}

= i
{parent=3g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

A <reliable transmission rate> $R$ means that length-$n$ codes can carry $M_n$ messages with $n^{-1}\log_2M_n$ tending to at least $R$ while the <probability measure> of the decoding-error event tends to zero. By the <Shannon second coding theorem>, the supremum of reliable rates is the <binary symmetric channel capacity>
$$
C(p)=1-h_2(p),
\qquad
h_2(p)=-p\log_2p-(1-p)\log_2(1-p).
$$
For small positive $p$, the <binary entropy function> has the <asymptotic expansion>
$$
h_2(p)=p\log_2\frac1p+\frac{p}{\ln2}+O(p^2),
$$
so $C(p)$ starts at one bit per channel use and decreases rapidly from one as errors are introduced.

= ii
{parent=3g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

At $p=1/2$, the output of the <binary symmetric channel> is <independent random variables>[independent] of its input. It conveys no <mutual information>, and therefore
$$
\boxed{C(1/2)=0.}
$$

= iii
{parent=3g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

If $p>1/2$, complementing every received bit converts the channel into a binary symmetric channel with crossover probability $1-p<1/2$. This deterministic and invertible output relabelling does not change the <channel capacity>. Since $h_2(p)=h_2(1-p)$,
$$
\boxed{C(p)=1-h_2(p)=C(1-p).}
$$
Thus the capacity rises again from zero as $p$ increases beyond $1/2$, reaching one at $p=1$, where every bit is flipped deterministically and can be recovered by complementation.

= 4H
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Automata and Formal Languages}

= a
{parent=4h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A <context-free grammar> is in <Chomsky normal form> when every production is of one of the forms
$$
A\to BC,
\qquad A\to a,
$$
where $A,B,C$ are <nonterminal symbols> and $a$ is a <terminal symbol>. Under this strict convention there is no <epsilon production>, so such a grammar cannot generate the <empty word>. Conversion of an arbitrary grammar therefore gives
$$
\boxed{\mathcal L(G_{\rm Chom})=\mathcal L(G)\setminus\{\epsilon\}.}
$$
If $\epsilon\notin\mathcal L(G)$, the two languages are equal. An alternative extended convention permits the exceptional start production $S_0\to\epsilon$; under that convention conversion preserves the whole language.

= b
{parent=4h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Use the <CYK algorithm>. For a nonempty input word $v=v_1\cdots v_n$, let $T_{i,j}$ be the set of nonterminals that derive the substring $v_i\cdots v_j$. Initialize
$$
T_{i,i}=\{A:A\to v_i\text{ is a production}\}.
$$
For increasing substring lengths, compute
$$
T_{i,j}=\bigcup_{k=i}^{j-1}
\{A:A\to BC,\ B\in T_{i,k},\ C\in T_{k+1,j}\}.
$$
Accept exactly when the start symbol belongs to $T_{1,n}$. The proof is a <mathematical induction> on substring length: a one-letter derivation uses a terminal production, while every longer <parse tree> has a root production $A\to BC$ whose two subtrees yield two adjacent nonempty substrings. The recurrence considers every possible split point and therefore finds exactly all possible roots. For a fixed grammar the algorithm uses $O(n^3)$ time. Under the strict form in part (a), it rejects $\epsilon$ immediately.

= c
{parent=4h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Introduce nonterminals for the terminals and one for the pair $bb$:
$$
A\to a,
\qquad B\to b,
\qquad C\to c,
\qquad U\to BB.
$$
Because the original start symbol occurs on a right-hand side, take a fresh start symbol $S_0$. After eliminating the <unit production> $S\to T$, one grammar in <Chomsky normal form> is
$$
\begin{aligned}
S_0&\to SU\mid AS\mid CC,\\
S&\to SU\mid AS\mid CC,\\
U&\to BB,\\
A&\to a,\qquad B\to b,\qquad C\to c.
\end{aligned}
$$
The original grammar generates exactly
$$
\{a^i cc b^{2j}:i,j\geq0\}.
$$
The displayed grammar generates the same set: $AS$ adds an $a$ on the left, $SU$ adds one $bb$ pair on the right, and $CC$ terminates the derivation with $cc$. Since the original grammar does not generate $\epsilon$,
$$
\boxed{\mathcal L(G)=\mathcal L(G_{\rm Chom}).}
$$

= 5J
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Statistical Modelling}

= a
{parent=5j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Writing the <log-likelihood> as $\ell(\beta)=\log L(\beta)$, the <Fisher information matrix> is the expected negative <Hessian matrix>
$$
\boxed{\mathcal I(\beta)=-\mathbb E_\beta\!\left[\nabla_\beta^2\ell(\beta)\right].}
$$
Under the usual regularity conditions it is equivalently the expected outer product of the <score function>.

= b
{parent=5j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For independent observations from an <exponential dispersion family> with common known <dispersion parameter> $\sigma^2$, the <generalized linear model> log-likelihood is
$$
\boxed{
\ell(\beta)
=\sum_{i=1}^n
\left[
\frac{y_i\theta_i-b(\theta_i)}{\sigma^2}
+c(y_i,\sigma^2)
\right],
}
$$
where $\mu_i=b'(\theta_i)$ and the <link function> satisfies $g(\mu_i)=\eta_i=x_i^T\beta$.

= c
{parent=5j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For the <canonical link function>, $\theta_i=\eta_i=x_i^T\beta$. Differentiating the log-likelihood gives the <score function>
$$
\nabla_\beta\ell(\beta)=\sigma^{-2}X^T(y-\mu).
$$
Since
$$
\frac{d\mu_i}{d\eta_i}=b''(\theta_i)=V(\mu_i),
$$
where $V$ is the <variance function>, a second differentiation gives
$$
\nabla_\beta^2\ell(\beta)
=-\sigma^{-2}X^T
\operatorname{diag}\bigl(V(\mu_1),\ldots,V(\mu_n)\bigr)X.
$$
This Hessian does not depend on the observed $y$, so taking its negative expectation changes nothing. Hence
$$
\boxed{\mathcal I(\beta)=\sigma^{-2}X^TWX,
\qquad W=\operatorname{diag}\bigl(V(\mu_1),\ldots,V(\mu_n)\bigr).}
$$

= 6C
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Mathematical Biology}

= Solution
{parent=6C}

With the <travelling wave> coordinate $\xi=x-ct$, the <reaction-diffusion system> becomes
$$
D f''+cf'+rf(1-f)=0,
\qquad f(-\infty)=1,quad f(+\infty)=0.
$$
At the advancing leading edge the healthy-tissue density is small. Put $f(\xi)\sim e^{-\lambda\xi}$ with $\lambda>0$. The <linearization> at $f=0$ gives
$$
D\lambda^2-c\lambda+r=0,
$$
so a nonoscillatory decaying front requires real positive roots,
$$
\lambda=\frac{c\pm\sqrt{c^2-4Dr}}{2D},
\qquad c\geq2\sqrt{Dr}.
$$
The <pulled travelling front> selects the <Fisher-KPP minimum wave speed>, and therefore
$$
\boxed{c_{\min}=2\sqrt{Dr}.}
$$

For the initial wound $0<x<W$, two mirror-image fronts enter from its endpoints. The left front moves right from $x=0$ and the right front moves left from $x=W$, each with speed approximately $c_{\min}$. A sequence of sketches would show the initial rectangular depression becoming two smooth monotone transition layers, the zero-density plateau shrinking, and the two layers meeting near $x=W/2$ before the profile relaxes to $S=1$. Their meeting time is
$$
\boxed{t_{\rm heal}\approx\frac{W/2}{c_{\min}}
=\frac{W}{4\sqrt{Dr}}.}
$$
There is an additional local relaxation time of order $1/r$ if “close to one” is assigned a fixed numerical tolerance, so the displayed estimate is the dominant healing time when $W$ is large compared with the front width $\sqrt{D/r}$.

= 7A
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Further Complex Methods}

= a
{parent=7a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Differentiating under the <contour integration> sign gives
$$
zw''+w=\int_\gamma e^{zt}\bigl(zt^2f(t)+f(t)\bigr)\,dt.
$$
Since $\partial_t e^{zt}=ze^{zt}$, <integration by parts> turns this into
$$
\left[e^{zt}t^2f(t)\right]_{\partial\gamma}
+\int_\gamma e^{zt}\left[f(t)-\frac{d}{dt}\bigl(t^2f(t)\bigr)\right]dt.
$$
The integral vanishes when
$$
(t^2f)'=f,
\qquad
\frac{f'}f=\frac1{t^2}-\frac2t.
$$
Hence, up to an irrelevant multiplicative constant,
$$
\boxed{f(t)=t^{-2}e^{-1/t}.}
$$
The remaining endpoint condition is
$$
\boxed{\left[e^{zt-1/t}\right]_{\partial\gamma}=0.}
$$
Thus each open end of $\gamma$ must lie where $\operatorname{Re}(zt-1/t)\to-\infty$; a closed contour also makes the boundary term vanish when the integrand is single-valued along it.

= b
{parent=7a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

One possible open contour begins at $t=0$ along a ray with $\operatorname{Re}(1/t)>0$, avoids the <essential singularity> at the origin, and ends at infinity along a ray with $\operatorname{Re}(zt)<0$. For example, when $z>0$ is real, it may leave the origin along the positive real axis, bend through the upper half-plane, and approach the negative real axis as $|t|\to\infty$. At its initial end, $e^{-1/t}\to0$; at its final end, $e^{zt}\to0$. More generally, the terminal ray may be chosen with
$$
\arg t=\pi-\arg z,
$$
so that $zt$ is negative real. This contour satisfies the endpoint condition from part (a) and therefore produces a solution of the differential equation.

= 8E
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Classical Dynamics}

= a
{parent=8e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Let
$$
\omega=\sqrt{\frac{k}{m}},
\qquad
H(x,p)=\frac{p^2}{2m}+\frac12m\omega^2x^2.
$$
An orbit of energy $E>0$ is an ellipse in <phase space>, with intercepts $x=\pm\sqrt{2E/(m\omega^2)}$ and $p=\pm\sqrt{2mE}$. Its enclosed area is $2\pi E/\omega$, so the <action variable> is
$$
I=\frac1{2\pi}\oint p\,dx=\frac E\omega.
$$
The transformation
$$
\boxed{x=\sqrt{\frac{2I}{m\omega}}\sin\theta,
\qquad
p=\sqrt{2m\omega I}\cos\theta}
$$
is canonical and gives the <action-angle variables>. Substitution yields
$$
\boxed{H(I)=\omega I,\qquad \dot I=0,\qquad\dot\theta=\omega.}
$$

= b
{parent=8e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <adiabatic invariance of the action> applies when the frequency changes by only a small fraction during one oscillation and no <separatrix> is encountered. A convenient condition is
$$
\left|\frac{\dot\omega}{\omega^2}\right|\ll1,
\qquad \omega(t)=\sqrt{\frac{k(t)}m}.
$$
Then
$$
\boxed{I=\frac E\omega\simeq\text{constant}.}
$$
Consequently
$$
E=I\omega\propto\sqrt{k}.
$$
If $A$ is the displacement amplitude, $E=\tfrac12kA^2$, and hence
$$
A=\sqrt{\frac{2I}{m\omega}}
\propto\omega^{-1/2}
\propto k^{-1/4}.
$$
Thus a slowly stiffened spring raises the energy as $k^{1/2}$ while reducing the amplitude as $k^{-1/4}$.

= 9B
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Cosmology}

= Solution
{parent=9B}

Let $M(r)$ be the <enclosed mass>. A spherical shell of thickness $dr$ has inward gravitational acceleration $GM(r)/r^2$, so <hydrostatic pressure support equation>[hydrostatic equilibrium] requires
$$
\boxed{\frac{dP}{dr}=-\frac{GM(r)\rho(r)}{r^2},
\qquad
\frac{dM}{dr}=4\pi r^2\rho(r).}
$$
With $\rho=Ar^2P$, these equations reduce to
$$
P'=-GAMP,
\qquad
M'=4\pi Ar^4P.
$$
For a regular central value $P(0)=P_c>0$, the first equation integrates to
$$
P(r)=P_c\exp\left(-GA\int_0^rM(s)\,ds\right).
$$
The <exponential function> is strictly positive at every finite $r$, so the pressure and density cannot reach zero at a finite stellar surface. The equations may describe an atmosphere whose density decreases at large radius, but they admit no nontrivial hydrostatic star of finite radius. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\text{there is no stable finite-radius stellar solution with this relation.}}
$$

= 10D
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Quantum Information and Computation}

= a
{parent=10d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Because $H_n|0^n\rangle=|\psi_0\rangle$,
$$
-H_nI_0H_n=2|\psi_0\rangle\langle\psi_0|-I,
$$
which is the <reflection operator> in the line spanned by $|\psi_0\rangle$. Meanwhile $I_G$ changes the sign of every good basis state and fixes every bad basis state; on
$$
\mathcal P=\operatorname{span}_{\mathbb R}\{|\psi_G\rangle,|\psi_B\rangle\}
$$
it is the reflection in the $|\psi_B\rangle$ axis. Both reflections preserve $\mathcal P$, so their product $Q$ does too.

Write
$$
|\psi_0\rangle
=\sqrt{\frac{k}{N}}|\psi_G\rangle
+\sqrt{\frac{N-k}{N}}|\psi_B\rangle
=\sin\theta|\psi_G\rangle+\cos\theta|\psi_B\rangle,
\qquad
\sin\theta=\sqrt{\frac{k}{N}}.
$$
In <Euclidean geometry>, the composition of reflections in two lines through the origin has a <rotation matrix> through twice the oriented angle between the lines. Hence $Q$ rotates the good-bad plane by $2\theta$ from $|\psi_B\rangle$ toward $|\psi_G\rangle$. In particular, the <Grover rotation angle> formula is
$$
\boxed{Q^r|\psi_0\rangle
=\sin((2r+1)\theta)|\psi_G\rangle
+\cos((2r+1)\theta)|\psi_B\rangle.}
$$

= b
{parent=10d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

When $k=N/4$,
$$
\sin\theta=\frac12,
\qquad \theta=\frac\pi6.
$$
One <Grover search algorithm> iteration therefore gives
$$
Q|\psi_0\rangle
=\sin(3\theta)|\psi_G\rangle+\cos(3\theta)|\psi_B\rangle
=|\psi_G\rangle.
$$
The selective sign change $I_G$ is implemented with one application of the <Boolean quantum oracle>: prepare its answer qubit in $|-\rangle=(|0\rangle-|1\rangle)/\sqrt2$ and use <quantum phase kickback>, obtaining $(-1)^{f(x)}$ on each $|x\rangle$. The remaining operations $H_n$, $I_0$, and the final computational-basis measurement are independent of $f$. Measurement of $|\psi_G\rangle$ returns some $x\in G$ with probability one, so exactly one query to $U_f$ suffices.

